{{also|pus}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|po͝os}}, {{IPA|/pʊs/}}, {{SAMPA|/pUs/}}
* {{rhymes|ʊs}}

===Etymology 1===
From a Common {{etyl|gem}} word for cat. Akin to {{etyl|nl|-}} ''[[poes#Dutch|poes]]'' "puss, cat", {{etyl|nds|-}} ''[[puuskatte#Low German|puus-katte]]'', Dial. {{etyl|sv|-}} ''[[kattepus#Swedish|kattepus]]'', {{etyl|nor|-}} ''[[pus#Norwegian|pus]]''

Found also in several other European and Western Asian languages. Cf. {{etyl|ron|-}} ''[[pisica]]''.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{informal}} A [[cat]]
#: ''Our local theatre is showing the pantomime ''Puss in Boots.
# a girl or young woman

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|cat}} [[moggie]]/[[moggy]]

=====Related terms=====
* [[pussy]]

===Etymology 2===
Of {{etyl|cel|en}} origin, from or akin to {{etyl|ga|en}} {{term|pus||lang=ga|mouth, lip}}, from {{etyl|mga|en}} {{term|bus||lang=mga}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{slang}} The [[mouth]]
#: ''She gave him a slap in the '''puss'''.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|mouth}} [[cakehole]], [[gob]], [[mush]], [[trap]]

===Anagrams===
* {{alphagram|pssu}}
* [[PSUs#English|PSUs]]
* [[sups#English|sups]]
* [[USPs#English|USPs]], [[USPS#English|USPS]]

